{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the earth was unformed and void.</b> What was, was — and what need is there for this knowledge? And it appears to say: for from the six days of Creation onward, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not change anything from the way it was, and the Holy One, blessed be He, foresaw that through the deeds of the wicked the world would revert to תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ as in the generation of the Flood. So too regarding the destruction of the Temple it is written (Jeremiah 4:23): ״רָאִיתִי אֶת הָאָרֶץ וְהִנֵּה תֹהוּ״, and Scripture informs us that if it should happen, at some time, that through the deeds of the wicked the world reverts to תֹהוּ, it would not be accounted a change in Creation, but the world would return to the way it was — for its nature is to be תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ וְחֹשֶׁךְ; and through the deeds of the righteous the Holy One, blessed be He, reverses its nature and created light for the righteous. And through the corruption of deeds the world returns to the way it was and it is not called a change in Creation, for the Holy One, blessed be He, stipulated a condition with every act of Creation. And for this reason He diminished the light and stored it away for the righteous — for He is not a man, HaShem, that He should relent — but rather because if the wicked are likened to darkness on account of the corruption of their deeds, it would not be accounted a change in Creation. And this is the view of the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 2:5): ״וְהָאָרֶץ הָיְתָה תֹהוּ״ — these are the deeds of the wicked; ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יְהִי אוֹר״ — these are the deeds of the righteous, etc. What did the author of this Midrash see to remove the verses from their plain sense? But according to what we have explained, the words of this Midrash are elucidated: for the plain sense remains in its place and the derashah is also derived from the plain sense itself. And what it concludes there, ״וְעֲדַיִן אֵינִי יוֹדֵעַ בְּאֵיזוֹ מֵהֶם חָפֵץ, אִם בְּמַעֲשֵׂיהֶם שֶׁל רְשָׁעִים״ etc. — you will find it explained in my small work called ״אֹרַח לְחַיִּים״ in the derashah for Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur; see there."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase ״מַאי דַּהֲוָה הֲוָה״ (literally 'what was, was') is rendered here as a rhetorical question about the necessity of the verse's information, preserving its idiomatic force."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:32:16.429539747Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}